{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '260,295p' PLAN.md && sed -n '545,718p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"With story that is about an hour. A test must prove every tool, hazard, strain, gift and the Code are\nexercised by the campaign (§11.2).\n\n**Strain scripts** (each is a state machine in code; its numbers are in the case's data)\n\n**Introit, 3/4.** A violet nodule moves under the tissue, seen as a bump. Every 3 beats it steps\nsomewhere new (a ripple telegraphs on beat 2) and leaves a laceration or pool behind. Scanner lights it\nup; Laser it at rest until the shell pops; Forceps pull it within 2 beats. First kill: it splits in two\n(60% heat each). Then four small quick ones on 2-beat steps. Last, an \"Amen\": they merge into a large\none held by 3 tethers (Scalpel), then Laser, then Forceps. The Hush wakes when vitals fall under 35 or\nat the second split, whichever is first: the meter fills, Bashir says \"Doctor... your hands,\" and the\nprompt appears.\n\n**Lacrimosa, 6/8.** A crown on the lung with 6 teardrop sacs on stems. Sacs swell for 8–12 beats and\nburst at full size into caustic pools (heavy drain, burns). Laser a stem (0.6 s) to drop a sac whole, or\nSuction it slowly for a lower rating. When a wave is cleared, the crown's eye opens for 4 beats: Scalpel\nacross it, twice. Three waves, each faster. Last, tear out the eye with Forceps and Gel the bed.\n\n**Dies Irae, 5/4.** A Fever meter fills (over 100: −6 vitals/s). Five embers around the pericardium heat\nthe tissue. Each 5th beat a heat ring sweeps the field: if your trigger is held as it crosses, you scorch\nthe tissue and lose your chain. Count-in and \"Lift off!\", a rhythm dodge. Cryoprobe an ember, Scalpel to\ncrack it, Forceps the 3 pieces out; each removal cools the Fever. Burns take Gel. Midway two embers merge\nand roam. Finally an incision reveals the furnace beneath: all tools in sequence.\n\n**Libera Me, shifting meter.** The heart beats visibly and hard. A: Introit nodules plus pools. B:\nLacrimosa sacs on the coronary arteries. C: embers and heat rings (7/8). D: the strain stops the heart:\nthree shocks, each on a ghost beat. E: the Soloist surfaces. Scalpel strokes appear on the beat, nearly a\nrhythm game, with a Duet window. Then a long tendril: Forceps, pulling only in diastole, a tug-of-war with\nthe pulse. Close with the Dressing. Teddy and Pet call it, since Bashir is the patient.\n\n**Code Blue.** A director spawns waves from the hazard set on one patient, with budget ×(0.6 + 0.4n) for n\nsurgeons and rising tempo every 30 s, ending in a small strain encounter. The team wins if the patient is\nalive at the end. Each surgeon has their own score, chain and seat colour. Also playable solo or local.\n\n---\n\n### 10.3 Two layers\n**Sim layer (`Game` hook, deterministic, testable with `pav`).** Cases *and* story live here as one phase machine. Scene\nnames: `surgery/campaign`, `surgery/case/<n>`, `surgery/codeblue`, `surgery/attract`, `surgery/title`, `surgery/card`,\n`surgery/mirror` (✔ the engine passes everything after the first `/` to your `build`).\nThe bot presses through story and plays operations, so **`surgery/campaign` is the scripted playthrough of the whole\ngame** and runs headless in seconds. Follow the engine's rules: `sim.state.rng` only, no clock, no `HashMap` order in\nstate, everything in your struct or entities, no threads or files in `pav_core`. Keep the struct compact: rewind clones\nit every 20 ticks. Use `dt × time_scale` in rules for the Hush, not the engine's speed control, so the cursor stays\nreal-time.\n\n**The bot** (`Game::bot`, one implementation) is a task planner: it picks the most urgent valid task, selects the tool, moves\nat a human cursor speed and performs the gesture, with noise scaled by `bot_skill`. It drives the tests, the balance tool,\nattract mode, the Case 7 partner and the Echo's ghost hands. Teach it each rule as you add the rule.\n\n**Shell layer (`pav_ui::surgery::Shell`, plain Rust plus egui).** Title, menus, pause, settings, dev, about, results input,\nattract timer. It must keep working while the sim is paused. It takes an abstract input (nav, confirm, back, tabs, pointer,\nlast device) and emits commands (load scene, resume, set tune value…). Draw it in both `pav_app` and\n`pav_ui::headless::Headless::frame`, so `pav ui` and `pav capture` see it and `cargo test` can drive it without a GPU.\n\n### 10.4 Engine seams you must open (the hook alone cannot draw any of this)\n1. `pav_core/src/frame.rs`: `RenderFrame.surgery: Option<Arc<SurgeryFrame>>`, filled in `Sim::frame`, exactly as\n   `RenderFrame.game` is for Shardfall. Add `SimEvent::Cue`.\n2. `pav_core/src/input.rs`: add `surgeons: [SurgeonInput; 4]` to `InputFrame` (it is `Copy` and recorded, so replays include\n   it) and a game command field beside the Shardfall-only `cmd`. `SurgeonInput { active, aim: Vec2, held, pressed, tool,\n   pressure: f32 }`. Extend the `input` tool (`surgeon slot=1 aim=[x,z] hold=use tool=3 ticks=30`).\n3. `pav_view/src/build.rs`: call `self.surgery.emit(&mut scene, curr, …)` where `self.arpg.emit` is called. Handle cues for\n   particles in `add_events`.\n4. **Camera:** there is no per-game camera. Put a `CameraShot { target, tilt, yaw, distance, fov, blend }` in `SurgeryFrame`\n   and apply it to `CameraRig` in both `pav_app` and `pav_tools/src/session.rs` (`render_ui`, `sync_camera`). `sim.state.focus`\n   is the camera target.\n5. `pav_core/src/scenes.rs`: `build_standalone_room` always spawns a player. In `build`, call `sim.despawn` on it ✔: that clears\n   `sim.state.player`, the sim steps and captures fine without one, and `sim.state.focus` then stays where you put it.\n   The level file holds only the OR wide-shot set, offset away from the field (e.g. `[layout] origin = [90, 0, -8]`), so it\n   is on screen only when a shot looks at it.\n6. **`pav_app/src/product.rs`: a separate frame path.** `App::frame` is about 800 lines of demo behaviour, and these must all\n   be off for this game ✔ (seen in the browser build): boot, stats and guide panels, room card, hint bar, toasts, touch layer,\n   F1–F12 keys, `H`, digits changing camera, wheel zoom, right-drag camera, **Backspace and Create rewinding the sim**, Options\n   opening the engine's dense pause menu with its virtual cursor, `Bindings` mapping Space to jump. Don't add forty conditions;\n   when the scene is `surgery*`, branch at the top of `frame` and `window_event` into your own code, reusing `Gfx`, `SimHost`,\n   `ViewBuilder` and `CameraRig`. Also hide the OS cursor in operations and replace \"Shardfall\" in window and error text.\n7. `pav_app/src/input.rs`: `poll_gamepad` reads only the first pad and thresholds triggers at 0.35. Add analog L2 and R2 and\n   per-pad state for up to 4 pads. ✔ gilrs reads a stubbed DualSense in the browser.\n8. `pav_ui/src/headless.rs` and `pav_tools/src/session.rs::has_ui`: include the shell and HUD. Leave `Game::hud` empty (those lines\n   are drawn as a debug box at the top left) and put state for tests in `Game::status`.\n9. `scripts/pack_single_html.py` and `web/index.html`: session client, tags, shim, about block; `--title`, `--description`,\n   `--theme-color` exist. **The packer rejects wasm-bindgen snippets ✔:** no `inline_js`. From Rust use `js_sys::Reflect` on\n   `window`.\n10. **Adding a dependency:** `build-web.sh` uses `--locked`. After changing a `Cargo.toml` (you will need `js-sys` in `pav_app`),\n    run `cargo check -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` once without `--locked` to refresh the lock.\n\n**Test hook:** from `pav_app`, set `window.__game` via `Reflect`: `status()` → JSON (screen, focus, device, prompt glyph ids,\nopen panels, sim status, bus gains and peaks), `step(n)` (run ticks now), `do(json)` (shell command). Browser frames are slow\nin software rendering ✔ (2–8 fps at 960×540 on this busy machine, at most 8 ticks a frame, then time is dropped), so tests must\nnot wait on real-time play.\n\n### 10.5 Data and designer tools\n\"The rest of the game could be built with normal designer tools\" means cases, story, plates, rigs and sets are text files a\ndesigner edits, with errors that name the file and line, and live reload.\n- **Case file:** `surgery`, `plate`, `ring`, `timer`, `par`, `vitals`, then `[[phase]]` with `spawn` lists, triggers (`on_clear`,\n  `at_time`, `vitals_below`, `on_beat`), `callouts`, `strain` + params, `reveal_plate`, `checkpoint = true`.\n- **Story file:** `[[line]] who, expr, text`, plus stage commands `set, enter, exit, music, cue, camera, flash, shake`.\n- Load through a `content_text(path, include_str!(…))` like `games::level_text` (disk first when native, so edits need no rebuild,\n  and embedded in the browser). A test checks every content file on disk is embedded.\n- Add `pav case_check path=…` (like `room_check`), and `pav balance` (§11.2).\n- `content/README.md`: how to add a case, strain, line of dialogue and character, in one page.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Testing\n\nRules from your TASK.md: one command, printing only failures and a one-line count; rerun only after changing what it covers;\nscreenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n### 11.1 `tests/run_all.py`\nRuns everything below with output captured, prints failures and `N passed, M failed`. `--rust` or `--web` for a part. Use the\nsystem `python3` **without `-I`** ✔ (`-I` hides Playwright). Keep one or two browsers open at most.\n\n### 11.2 Rust (fast, no GPU)\n- Bot wins every case and the whole campaign on 10 seeds at skill 1.0 (`bot_skill` scales speed and accuracy).\n- A careless player (idle; random tool spam) loses every case.\n- Determinism: snapshot, rewind and replay hashes are exact (copy `engine/crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs`).\n- **Coverage:** counters in the campaign prove every tool, hazard, strain phase, gift and Code fired, and every rating was reachable.\n- Rule units: each tool's rating thresholds, wrong tool, chain, ranks, beat window, Code, retry checkpoint.\n- Content lint: every file parses, every referenced speaker, expression, plate and cue exists, no empty text, no leftover \"TODO\".\n- **Balance:** `pav balance seeds=20 skills=1.0,0.7,0.4` prints wins, time, vitals left and suggested `par` per case. Target: 1.0 always\n  wins at S or above; 0.7 wins most; 0.4 loses later cases; 0 always loses. Tune with this rather than by feel alone.\n- Shell: pad, keyboard and mouse navigation through every screen, hover moves focus, prompts follow last device, Xbox setting, attract\n  starts at 10 s and exits on any input, touch-only state, dev sliders apply and reset, About shows every required section.\n- Audio: music default gain is 0.10; every cue and voice renders non-silent offline; each track renders; extend `audio_capture`.\n\n### 11.3 Browser (built single file, `file://`)\nLaunch Chromium with exactly ✔ `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader\n--use-angle=swiftshader`. With these, canvas screenshots show real pixels ✔. Check through `window.__game`:\n- Boots with no console errors; right title; none of the engine's overlays.\n- Stubbed DualSense (Appendix D) ✔: menus, analog R2 reaches the sim, flick selects tools, Options pauses. Keyboard and mouse: tools, wheel, hover,\n  click. Prompts flip between devices.\n- Attract at 10 s and exit on key, pad and mouse.\n- Phone profile (Chromium device profile, tap with `page.touchscreen`): message, Settings button, no touch controls, attract runs. Also load a\n  WebKit iPhone profile as far as it goes (it has no WebGPU here, so expect the page's WebGPU message).\n- Audio: analyser tap (Appendix D) ✔ reads non-silence; `status()` shows music 0.10.\n- Settings persist across a reload.\n- `step` the whole `surgery/campaign` in the browser with the bot and reach Won.\n- Screenshots at 1920×1080 of title, op, story, results, dev, about into `tests/out/`; look at them when visuals change. For quick looks, `pav capture`\n  and `filmstrip` are cheaper.\n\n### 11.4 Hub\nCopy the game next to `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` in `tests/hub/`. Serve with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1 -d tests/hub`.\n**Use `-u` ✔: without it the port line is buffered when piped and your script hangs forever.** Read the port from the first line, and check that the\npage is your game. Stop only the server you started, by PID; many other agents' servers and browsers run on this machine ✔. Wait for\n`document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, then require `window.__hubCheck.pass`, for each query:\n`players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, the same with `&scenario=host-leave`, `players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`, `players=1&auto=1` (add\n`&limit=20` for speed). Then a two-copy test reading each frame's `__game.status()`: a client's input moves its cursor on the host; both see the same\nvitals. Then open outside a session: title appears, the game is unaffected.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Build order\n\nKeep it playable after every step. Build the slot and frame architecture from step 1 so co-op needs no rewrite.\n\n1. **Seams.** Set up (§10.1). Scene `surgery/case/0`, `SurgeryFrame`, product frame path, camera shot, plate rendering, one surgeon, forceps on a hook,\n   rating text, vitals, heart clock. Bot wins it; tests run; browser boots with the shim's session client in place. Check the look at 1920×1080.\n2. **Hands.** All instruments and hazards, ring, aim assist, chain, results, death and retry, tray, full HUD, cues. Cases 0–2 as data. Bot, coverage and balance tools.\n3. **Shell.** Title, menus, pause, settings, save, prompts and glyphs, 10-foot style, touch-only, attract, dev mode. Browser tests.\n4. **Theatre and sound.** Paper rigs, sets, story player, voices, music with buses; story for Cases 0–3.\n5. **Strains and gifts.** Introit and Hush, Lacrimosa, Echo and tremor, bone reassembly, Dies Irae and Cryoprobe, Libera Me and Duet. Cases 3–7 with their stories.\n6. **Together.** Multi-pad local co-op, Code Blue, partner bot, hub bridge, card, mirror scene, network. Hub tests.\n7. **Finish.** Balance every case. Polish passes: an audio mix pass, camera punches, particles, transitions. Designer guide. Final package.\n\nIf you run short, a smaller finished game beats a bigger unfinished one. Cut in this order: fewer sets, then fewer waves in a strain, then Case 4 or 5's\nlength. **Never cut:** any tool, gift or screen in this plan, or Case 3 and Case 7.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Finish line\n\n**Package.** One self-contained HTML at the path your TASK.md names, built with `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene surgery/title --title … --description …\n--theme-color … --out …`, with fonts and everything else embedded. It must run from `file://` in Chrome or Edge; browsers without WebGPU get the page's clear message\n(call `AIHub.fail` in a session). Delete `engine/target` when finished, so only source remains in your dev folder.\n\n**Feedback.** Keep notes as you go on what tests caught and what you read or changed in the engine: your TASK.md asks for counts and specifics at the end. Follow its exit\ninterview shape exactly and check it parses. Suggested catalogue values (name and tagline are yours): type `Game`; genre `Simulation`; source as your TASK.md's; series `Trauma Center`; players `1–4`; controls such as \"Right stick or mouse aims, R2 or left click operates, left stick or 1–8 picks tools\"; tags from `3d, story, co-op,\narcade, boss fights, comedy`. Regenerate the About block from those answers and rebuild last, then rerun the web tests.\n\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubs; every test passes; the campaign and every case can be finished by a person with a pad, or with a mouse.\n\n### Requirements map\n\n| Your TASK.md asks for | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective | §1 |\n| Title riff chosen by RNG, single constant | Top note; you do this |\n| Single HTML package, dev subfolder | §10.1–10.2, §13 |\n| Build on the engine, change anything needed | §10 |\n| Art and animation on the engine's native style, new assets | §6, §7 |\n| Audio in code, gibberish voices, music 10% | §8, §5 Settings |\n| AI Hub online, plus the game on its own | §9 |\n| Shared machine rules (ports, PIDs, folders) | §10.1, §11 |\n| Unlimited performance budget | §6 |\n| Headless tests, one command | §11 |\n| Original game; world, characters, themes, tone → mechanics | §1–§4 |\n| Feature-complete vertical slice showing all mechanics | §3, §4, §11.2 coverage |\n| Infrastructure for later content with designer tools | §10.5 |\n| Dev mode from pause, sliders, reset | §5.2 |\n| Attract mode | §5 |\n| Narrative tone | §2 |\n| 10-foot, DualSense first; native mouse and keyboard; prompts follow input; Xbox glyph setting; no dense screens | §3.2, §5 |\n| Touch-only message, settings, attract | §5 |\n| No stubs | §13 |\n| Source Information and feedback in chat and About | §5 About, §13 |\n| Exit interview | §13 |\n\n---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The randomly selected title is **Trauma Refrain: Third Opinion**. I’m keeping the eight-case story and all eight instruments, with shorter strain waves where needed to make the demo manageable. I’ll check the engine’s extension points next so the surgical scenes can use its cel shading, lighting, and animation.\n"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall.git \"$(cat .game-path)/engine\"'","aggregated_output":"Cloning into 'Trauma-Refrain-Third-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-gpt-6.1-sol-max-20261105-015057/engine'...\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'd=$(cat .game-path); cat \"$d/engine/START_HERE.md\"; cat \"$d/engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md\"; cat \"$d/engine/AGENTS.md\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'd=$(cat .game-path); cat \"$d/engine/START_HERE.md\"; cat \"$d/engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md\"; cat \"$d/engine/AGENTS.md\"'","aggregated_output":"# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n\nA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\nand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\ncaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n\n## Which route\n- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n\nPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n\n> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n\n> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n\nAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\nsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n\n## Linux setup\nUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\nRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\ntoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n\nOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\nalready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\nto install missing packages.\n\n```sh\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n```\n\nSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\nMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\nand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n\n## Build and inspect headlessly\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n./target/debug/pav help\n./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n```\n\n`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\ntown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\nkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n\n```sh\nprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\ncargo test --locked -p pav_core\n```\n\nInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\nevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\nbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\nwait for a server.\n\n## Run the game window\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n```\n\nWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\nheadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\nsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n\nFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\nadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n\n## The studios (live editing in the window)\n- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n\nEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\nseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n\n## Make another handoff archive\nFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\ninto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\nfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\nunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\nwould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n# Make a game on Pavilion\n\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n\n## What you get\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n  and replays are exact.\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n  one self-contained HTML file).\n\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n**starter game**.\n\n## Read this, skip that\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n\n## Quick start\n```sh\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n```\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n\n## Your game in six steps\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n\n## The level: a room file\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\ncorridor. In a room file:\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n  the level: `pav params`).\n\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n\nimpl Game for MyGame {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n}\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n```\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n\n## Building blocks\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n\n## Rules that keep it working\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n|---|---|\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n\n## Ship it\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n\n## Limits today\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n\n## Report back\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\n# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n\nMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\nyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n\nShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\nprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\nthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\nread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\ntool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\nneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\nread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\nCLI examples.\n\n## How the doctrine applies here\nAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\nfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n  log it and continue.\n- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n\n## Working rules\nUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n\n## Testing budget (prototype stage)\nEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\nmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\nrecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n  errors.\n- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n  are noise.\n- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n  the user on a quick comparison page.\n- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n  a verified benefit.\n- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n  whole context.\n- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n\n## Architecture\nCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n\nData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\nmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\nData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n\nKey rules:\n- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n\n## Build, run, test\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\ncargo build                        # everything (Linux)\ncargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\ncargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\ncargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\ncargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\nscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\nscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\nscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\nscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\nscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n```\n`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\nother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\nBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\nsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\nbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\nChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\nin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\nDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\nincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n(or run `cargo clean`).\n\n## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\nEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\nMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\nthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\nand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n```sh\ncargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\npav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\npav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\nprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\nprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n```\n- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n  use them for bug reports.\n- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n  assets in game code\").\n- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n\nAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\nIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n\nTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\nspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\nroom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\nlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\nloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\nlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\nanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\nasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\ncreature_status`.\n\n### Adding a tool\n1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n\n### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\nCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\nis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\nteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\nfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\nhot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\nfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n\n### Scenes\n`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\nstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\nMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\nGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\nCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\nextend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\nlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n\n## Conventions\n- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n  the PR); don't ask first.\n- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n\n## Code Review Rules\nFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\nLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'd=$(cat .game-path); mkdir -p \"$d/vendor\"; curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md -o \"$d/vendor/hub-README.md\"; cat \"$d/vendor/hub-README.md\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'd=$(cat .game-path); mkdir -p \"$d/vendor\"; curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md -o \"$d/vendor/hub-README.md\"; cat \"$d/vendor/hub-README.md\"'","aggregated_output":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'd=$(cat .game-path); sed -n '\"'30,150p' \\\"\"'$d/engine/AGENTS.md\"; cat \"$d/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs\"; cat \"$d/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'd=$(cat .game-path); sed -n '\"'30,150p' \\\"\"'$d/engine/AGENTS.md\"; cat \"$d/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs\"; cat \"$d/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs\"'","aggregated_output":"  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n  log it and continue.\n- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n\n## Working rules\nUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n\n## Testing budget (prototype stage)\nEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\nmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\nrecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n  errors.\n- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n  are noise.\n- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n  the user on a quick comparison page.\n- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n  a verified benefit.\n- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n  whole context.\n- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n\n## Architecture\nCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n\nData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\nmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\nData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n\nKey rules:\n- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n\n## Build, run, test\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\ncargo build                        # everything (Linux)\ncargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\ncargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\ncargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\ncargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\nscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\nscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\nscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\nscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\nscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n```\n`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\nother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\nBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\nsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\nbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\nChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\n//! Games built on the engine: the hook a new game implements, and the list of games.\n//!\n//! A game is a struct holding its rules' state (`Clone` and serde, like everything in\n//! `SimState`) that implements [`Game`], plus a `build` function that makes its scene. One\n//! line in [`GAMES`] and `load scene=<key>` starts it in the tools, the window\n//! (`shardfall --scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`). `starter.rs` is the template\n//! to copy; `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` is the guide. Shardfall (`crate::arpg`) predates this hook and\n//! keeps its own.\n\nuse std::any::Any;\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nuse anyhow::Result;\nuse serde::de::{DeserializeOwned, Error as _};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize, Serializer};\n\nuse crate::entity::EntityId;\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\npub mod starter;\n\n/// Every game the engine knows. `load scene=<key>` (or `<key>/<rest>`) builds it.\npub static GAMES: &[Entry] =\n    &[entry::<starter::Starter>(\"Starter: collect every shard while slimes chase you (the template to copy).\", starter::build)];\n\n/// Still playing, won or lost.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\npub enum Outcome {\n    #[default]\n    Playing,\n    Won,\n    Lost,\n}\n\n/// A game's rules. Every method has a default, so a game implements only what it uses.\n///\n/// The engine calls `pre` and `post` once per tick (60 a second) from `Sim::step`; everything\n/// else is read-only. While `pre` or `post` runs, the game is out of `sim.state.play`, so use\n/// `self` for the game's own state and `sim` for the world.\npub trait Game: Clone + Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send + Sync + 'static {\n    /// The scene key that starts the game: `load scene=<KEY>`, `--scene <KEY>`, `?scene=<KEY>`.\n    const KEY: &'static str;\n\n    /// Before characters move: read the player's input and decide what the characters the\n    /// game drives do this tick. A returned input replaces that character's own (its `ai`\n    /// brain, or the player's keys for the player).\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        _sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        BTreeMap::new()\n    }\n\n    /// After physics: the rules (pickups, damage, score, win and loss). Push `SimEvent`s for\n    /// sounds and effects (`Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`...).\n    fn post(&mut self, _sim: &mut Sim, _dt: f32, _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {}\n\n    /// HUD lines: drawn at the top left of the window, returned by the `play` tool.\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        Vec::new()\n    }\n\n    /// The game's state for tools and tests (`play`), as JSON.\n    fn status(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        serde_json::Value::Null\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        Outcome::Playing\n    }\n\n    /// The input a good player would give this tick. `play bot=true` and the tests drive the\n    /// player with it; teach it every new rule, and keep a test that it wins.\n    fn bot(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        InputFrame::default()\n    }\n}\n\n/// One line of [`GAMES`]: made with [`entry`].\npub struct Entry {\n    pub key: &'static str,\n    pub about: &'static str,\n    /// Builds the scene and sets `sim.state.play`. The `&str` is the rest of the scene name\n    /// after `<key>/` (a level number, say), if any.\n    pub build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>,\n    load: fn(ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String>,\n}\n\n/// A [`GAMES`] line for the game type `G`.\npub const fn entry<G: Game>(about: &'static str, build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>) -> Entry {\n    Entry { key: G::KEY, about, build, load: load_as::<G> }\n}\n\nfn load_as<G: Game>(v: ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String> {\n    v.deserialized::<G>().map(GameBox::new).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n}\n\n/// A game's level text: `crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` read from disk when the program\n/// runs from the repository's root (`pav`, `cargo run`), so an edit shows at the next `load`\n/// with no rebuild; else `built_in`, the copy embedded with `include_str!` (tests, the browser,\n/// a shipped executable).\npub fn level_text(key: &str, built_in: &'static str) -> std::borrow::Cow<'static, str> {\n    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\n    if let Ok(text) = std::fs::read_to_string(format!(\"crates/pav_core/src/games/{key}.toml\")) {\n        return text.into();\n    }\n    #[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]\n    let _ = key; // the browser has no files\n    built_in.into()\n}\n\n/// The game registered under `key`.\npub fn find(key: &str) -> Option<&'static Entry> {\n    GAMES.iter().find(|g| g.key == key)\n}\n\n/// A running game in `SimState::play`: clones (rewind, snapshots) and serializes (snapshot\n/// files, the state hash) through the game's own derives.\npub struct GameBox(Box<dyn DynGame>);\n\nimpl GameBox {\n    pub fn new<G: Game>(game: G) -> Self {\n        Self(Box::new(game))\n    }\n\n    pub fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n        self.0.key()\n    }\n\n    /// The game as its own type (None for another game).\n    pub fn get<G: Game>(&self) -> Option<&G> {\n        self.0.as_any().downcast_ref()\n    }\n\n    pub fn get_mut<G: Game>(&mut self) -> Option<&mut G> {\n        self.0.as_any_mut().downcast_mut()\n    }\n\n    pub fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        self.0.hud(sim)\n    }\n\n    pub fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        self.0.status(sim)\n    }\n\n    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.0.outcome()\n    }\n\n    pub fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        self.0.bot(sim)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Clone for GameBox {\n    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n        Self(self.0.clone_box())\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\nstruct Saved {\n    key: String,\n    state: ciborium::Value,\n}\n\nimpl Serialize for GameBox {\n    fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {\n        let state = self.0.save().map_err(serde::ser::Error::custom)?;\n        Saved { key: self.key().to_string(), state }.serialize(s)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for GameBox {\n    fn deserialize<D: Deserializer<'de>>(d: D) -> Result<Self, D::Error> {\n        let saved = Saved::deserialize(d)?;\n        let Some(entry) = find(&saved.key) else {\n            let known: Vec<&str> = GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key).collect();\n            return Err(D::Error::custom(format!(\"unknown game '{}' (known: {})\", saved.key, known.join(\", \"))));\n        };\n        (entry.load)(saved.state).map_err(D::Error::custom)\n    }\n}\n\n/// [`Game`] as an object (the trait itself isn't one: `Clone` and serde).\ntrait DynGame: Send + Sync {\n    fn key(&self) -> &'static str;\n    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame>;\n    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String>;\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>);\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value;\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;\n    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any;\n    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any;\n}\n\nimpl<G: Game> DynGame for G {\n    fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n        G::KEY\n    }\n    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame> {\n        Box::new(self.clone())\n    }\n    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String> {\n        ciborium::Value::serialized(self).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n    }\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        Game::pre(self, sim, input, dt, events)\n    }\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        Game::post(self, sim, dt, events)\n    }\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        Game::hud(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        Game::status(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        Game::outcome(self)\n    }\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        Game::bot(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any {\n        self\n    }\n    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any {\n        self\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Sim {\n    /// The running game from [`GAMES`], if this scene is one.\n    pub fn play(&self) -> Option<&GameBox> {\n        self.state.play.as_ref()\n    }\n\n    /// The running game's outcome (`Playing` when there is none).\n    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.outcome()).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// The running game's bot input (none without a game).\n    pub fn bot_input(&self) -> InputFrame {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.bot(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_pre(&mut self, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return BTreeMap::new() };\n        let out = g.0.pre(self, input, dt, events);\n        self.state.play = Some(g);\n        out\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_post(&mut self, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return };\n        g.0.post(self, dt, events);\n        self.state.play = Some(g);\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_hud(&self) -> Vec<String> {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.hud(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// Plays the running game with its bot until it is won or lost, or `ticks` pass.\n    /// Returns the outcome and the ticks played.\n    pub fn run_bot(&mut self, ticks: u64) -> (Outcome, u64) {\n        for t in 0..ticks {\n            let o = self.outcome();\n            if o != Outcome::Playing {\n                return (o, t);\n            }\n            let input = self.bot_input();\n            self.step(&input);\n        }\n        (self.outcome(), ticks)\n    }\n}\n//! The starter game, and the template for a new one: collect every shard while slimes chase\n//! you round the walls. Three lives; a slime's touch sends everyone back to the start.\n//!\n//! To start a game of your own, copy this file and `starter.toml` to `<key>.rs` and\n//! `<key>.toml`, change `KEY`, add its line to `GAMES` in `mod.rs`, then change the rules.\n//! `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` walks through it.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow};\nuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\nuse serde_json::json;\n\nuse super::{Game, GameBox, Outcome};\nuse crate::color::Color;\nuse crate::entity::{Behavior, EntityId, Spawn};\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::nav::NavGrid;\nuse crate::room::RoomDef;\nuse crate::shape::{Shape, Visual};\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\n/// The level, a room file, built in (the browser has no files). Running from the repository,\n/// `level_text` reads the file on disk instead, so an edit shows at the next `load`.\nconst LEVEL: &str = include_str!(\"starter.toml\");\n\n/// The rules' numbers: the level's `[game]` table, each with a default, so balancing is an\n/// edit to the level and a `load` (no rebuild).\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]\npub struct Rules {\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Slime speed as a fraction of a walker's top speed (each slime gets ±15%, from the seed).\n    pub slime_speed: f32,\n    /// Slimes chase a player within this walking distance (m).\n    pub slime_sight: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) catch the player.\n    pub catch: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) to a shard pick it up.\n    pub pick: f32,\n    /// Seconds the player is safe at the start and after a catch.\n    pub grace: f32,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Rules {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { lives: 3, slime_speed: 0.5, slime_sight: 9.0, catch: 0.8, pick: 0.9, grace: 2.0 }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct Starter {\n    pub rules: Rules,\n    /// Shards still on the floor.\n    pub shards: Vec<EntityId>,\n    pub total: usize,\n    pub slimes: Vec<EntityId>,\n    /// Each slime's start (feet) and speed.\n    pub homes: Vec<Vec3>,\n    pub speeds: Vec<f32>,\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Seconds the player is still safe from slimes.\n    pub grace: f32,\n    pub time: f32,\n    pub outcome: Outcome,\n    /// The walkable grid. It follows from the level alone, so it isn't saved: rebuilt when\n    /// missing (after a snapshot load).\n    #[serde(skip)]\n    nav: Option<Arc<NavGrid>>,\n}\n\n/// Builds the level, places the shards on its `shard` markers and starts the game.\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, _rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()> {\n    let def = RoomDef::parse(&super::level_text(Starter::KEY, LEVEL)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml: {e}\"))?;\n    let rules: Rules = toml::Value::Table(def.game.clone()).try_into().map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml [game]: {e}\"))?;\n    crate::scenes::build_standalone_room(sim, Starter::KEY, def);\n    let mut shards = Vec::new();\n    for at in crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\") {\n        let mut look = Visual::new(Shape::Box { half: Vec3::splat(0.2) }, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"));\n        look.emissive = 0.8;\n        let shard = Spawn::new(\"shard\", at + Vec3::Y * 0.7).visual(look).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed: 2.5 });\n        shards.push(sim.spawn(shard));\n    }\n    // Slimes come from the level's [[npc]] entries; the game drives them.\n    let slimes: Vec<EntityId> = sim.state.entities.iter().filter(|e| e.name == \"slime\").map(|e| e.id).collect();\n    let homes = slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n    // Randomness comes from the sim's seeded generator, so a seed always plays the same.\n    let speeds = slimes.iter().map(|_| rules.slime_speed * sim.state.rng.range(0.85, 1.15)).collect();\n    let game = Starter {\n        total: shards.len(),\n        shards,\n        slimes,\n        homes,\n        speeds,\n        lives: rules.lives,\n        grace: rules.grace,\n        rules,\n        time: 0.0,\n        outcome: Outcome::Playing,\n        nav: None,\n    };\n    sim.state.play = Some(GameBox::new(game));\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nimpl Game for Starter {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"starter\";\n\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let mut out = BTreeMap::new();\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            // Decided: everyone stands still.\n            for &id in sim.state.player.iter().chain(&self.slimes) {\n                out.insert(id, InputFrame::default());\n            }\n            return out;\n        }\n        if self.nav.is_none() {\n            self.nav = NavGrid::for_room(sim).map(Arc::new);\n        }\n        let Some(nav) = self.nav.clone() else { return out };\n        // One flow field toward the player steers every slime round the walls.\n        let player = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id));\n        let field = player.filter(|_| self.grace <= 0.0).and_then(|p| nav.flow(p, self.rules.slime_sight));\n        for (&id, &speed) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.speeds) {\n            let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n            if let (Some(field), Some(at)) = (&field, sim.feet(id))\n                && let Some(d) = field.dir(&nav, at)\n            {\n                input.move_dir = Vec2::new(d.x, d.z) * speed;\n            }\n            out.insert(id, input);\n        }\n        out\n    }\n\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            return;\n        }\n        self.time += dt;\n        self.grace = (self.grace - dt).max(0.0);\n        let Some(pid) = sim.state.player else { return };\n        let Some(p) = sim.feet(pid) else { return };\n\n        // Shards: walk over one to pick it up.\n        let near: Vec<(EntityId, Vec3)> = self\n            .shards\n            .iter()\n            .filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| (id, e.pos)))\n            .filter(|(_, at)| flat(*at - p) < self.rules.pick)\n            .collect();\n        for (id, at) in near {\n            self.shards.retain(|&s| s != id);\n            sim.despawn(id);\n            events.push(SimEvent::Coin { pos: at });\n            let left = self.shards.len();\n            sim.say(if left == 1 { \"1 shard to go\".to_string() } else { format!(\"{left} shards to go\") });\n        }\n        if self.shards.is_empty() {\n            self.outcome = Outcome::Won;\n            sim.say(format!(\"All shards in {:.1} s!\", self.time));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // Slimes: a touch costs a life and sends everyone back to the start.\n        let catch = self.rules.catch;\n        let caught = self.grace <= 0.0 && self.slimes.iter().any(|&id| sim.feet(id).is_some_and(|s| flat(s - p) < catch));\n        if caught {\n            self.lives -= 1;\n            events.push(SimEvent::Hit { pos: p + Vec3::Y, strength: 1.0, target: Some(pid) });\n            if self.lives == 0 {\n                self.outcome = Outcome::Lost;\n                sim.say(\"Caught! Game over\");\n                return;\n            }\n            sim.say(format!(\"Caught! {} lives left\", self.lives));\n            let spawn = sim.state.spawn;\n            sim.set_position(pid, spawn);\n            for (&id, &home) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.homes) {\n                sim.set_position(id, home);\n            }\n            self.grace = self.rules.grace;\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        let mut lines =\n            vec![format!(\"Shards {}/{}\", self.total - self.shards.len(), self.total), format!(\"Lives {}\", self.lives)];\n        match self.outcome {\n            Outcome::Won => lines.push(format!(\"You win! {:.1} s\", self.time)),\n            Outcome::Lost => lines.push(\"Game over\".into()),\n            Outcome::Playing => {}\n        }\n        lines\n    }\n\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        let xz = |id: EntityId| sim.feet(id).map(|f| [round(f.x), round(f.z)]);\n        json!({\n            \"outcome\": self.outcome,\n            \"time\": round(self.time),\n            \"shards_left\": self.shards.len(),\n            \"total\": self.total,\n            \"lives\": self.lives,\n            \"grace\": round(self.grace),\n            \"player\": sim.state.player.and_then(xz),\n            \"shards\": self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| [round(e.pos.x), round(e.pos.z)])).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n            \"slimes\": self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| xz(id)).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.outcome\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the shortest walk to a shard that keeps clear of slimes (cells near them count\n    /// as walls); with every shard cut off, makes for the reachable spot farthest from them.\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n        let Some(p) = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id)) else { return input };\n        let Some(mut grid) = self.nav.as_deref().cloned().or_else(|| NavGrid::for_room(sim)) else { return input };\n        let slimes: Vec<Vec3> = self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n        if self.grace < 0.5 {\n            for i in 0..grid.blocked.len() {\n                if slimes.iter().any(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s) < 1.8) {\n                    grid.blocked[i] = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let paths = self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id)).filter_map(|e| grid.path(p, e.pos));\n        let path = paths.min_by(|a, b| length(p, a).total_cmp(&length(p, b))).or_else(|| {\n            let clearance = |i: usize| slimes.iter().map(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s)).fold(f32::MAX, f32::min);\n            let reach = grid.flow(p, 6.0)?;\n            let safest = (0..grid.blocked.len())\n                .filter(|&i| reach.dist[i].is_finite())\n                .max_by(|&a, &b| clearance(a).total_cmp(&clearance(b)))?;\n            grid.path(p, grid.center(safest))\n        });\n        input.move_dir = path.map(|path| flat_dir(path[0] - p)).unwrap_or_default();\n        input\n    }\n}\n\nfn flat(v: Vec3) -> f32 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).length()\n}\n\nfn flat_dir(v: Vec3) -> Vec2 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).normalize_or_zero()\n}\n\n/// Walking length of a path from `p`.\nfn length(p: Vec3, path: &[Vec3]) -> f32 {\n    let (mut at, mut sum) = (p, 0.0);\n    for &q in path {\n        sum += flat(q - at);\n        at = q;\n    }\n    sum\n}\n\nfn round(x: f32) -> f64 {\n    (x as f64 * 100.0).round() / 100.0\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
